  "query": "what is Chainlink",
  "top_score": 0.839,
  "results": [
    {
      "title": "Chainlink (LINK)",
      "section": "What it is",
      "score": 0.839,
      "text": "## What it is\n\nChainlink is not a standalone blockchain \u2014 it is middleware. Networks\nof node operators retrieve data from external sources (price feeds,\nweather data, sports results, payment confirmations) and deliver them\non-chain in a verifiable way. Smart contracts on Ethereum and other\nchains use Chainlink data feeds to make decisions about prices,\nliquidations, settlements, and more."
    },
    {
      "title": "Chainlink (LINK)",
      "section": "Why it exists",
      "score": 0.836,
      "text": "## Why it exists\n\nSmart contracts cannot directly access data outside their own chain.\nChainlink was created to solve this \"oracle problem\" by providing a\ndecentralised, tamper-resistant way to bring real-world information\non-chain \u2014 the foundation for any DeFi protocol that needs accurate\nprices, any insurance protocol that needs event data, and any tokenised\nasset that mirrors something off-chain."
    },
    {
      "title": "Chainlink (LINK)",
      "section": "headline",
      "score": 0.804,
      "text": "# Chainlink (LINK)\n\nChainlink is a decentralised oracle network that connects blockchains\nto off-chain data, computation, and external systems. It is the\ndominant oracle provider in the smart-contract ecosystem."
    },
    {
      "title": "Chainlink (LINK)",
      "section": "Notable context",
      "score": 0.799,
      "text": "## Notable context\n\nChainlink's price feeds secure the majority of DeFi total value locked.\nIts Cross-Chain Interoperability Protocol (CCIP), launched in 2023,\nextends its role into messaging between chains. Despite this central\ninfrastructural role, LINK has often underperformed during crypto bull\nruns, with critics pointing to slow token utility growth relative to\nnetwork usage."
    }
  ]
}

